\DOC LOCAL_MUL_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal across one inlined
multiplication subroutine, replacing the current state variable by a fresh
successor state and installing the subroutine's arithmetic specification as new
assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index selecting which code block to step
over (the same value throughout the observed traces); the second is a
{string list} naming the result location and the two operand locations of the
multiplication, which fix the name of the new result variable and the operand
terms appearing in the generated bound and congruence assumptions. The
conclusion keeps its shape, with its trailing state term replaced by a new
state variable one step later. All state-dependent assumptions about the old
state (program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory contents, and
the accumulated frame-change assumption) are discharged and re-added for the
new state: the memory cell named by the first string now holds a freshly named
result value, the program counter is updated, and the frame-change assumption
is possibly extended by further components. Two new state-independent
assumptions are added, giving a bound on the result and a congruence relating
it to the product of the two operands. State-independent assumptions already
present are retained unchanged, though their position in the list may shift.
{
      A ?- t
      ==========================================  LOCAL_MUL_4_TAC n ["res"; "op1"; "op2"]
      A' ?- t'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the old state's assumptions replaced by their
successor-state counterparts plus the two new arithmetic facts, and {t'} is {t}
with the trailing state term replaced by the successor state.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, applied to
goals of the same machine-execution form.

\ENDDOC
